      "text": "To find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we start by using the given information that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) for all \\(x\\). First, we need to express \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Given \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\):\n\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\n\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can equate the two expressions:\n\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\n\nFor the equation to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. This gives us the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 5 = 4\n\\]\n\nWe solve the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
