      "text": "We are given that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\). First, let's express \\( g(f(x)) \\) in terms of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\). We know that \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) and \\( g(x) = 2x - 5 \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\nWe are also given that \\( g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\). So we can set the two expressions equal to each other:\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\nFor this equation to hold for all \\( x \\), the coefficients of \\( x \\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. This gives us two equations:\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 5 = 4\n\\]\nWe solve the first equation for \\( a \\):\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nNext, we solve the second equation for \\( b \\):\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\nNow we have \\( a = \\frac{3}{2} \\) and \\( b = \\frac{9}{2} \\). To find \\( a + b \\), we add these values together:\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3+9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of \\( a + b \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
