      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that the composition of the functions \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\) satisfies the equation \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) for all \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) \\]\nSince \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we have:\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\), so we can set the expressions equal to each other:\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\nFor this equation to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides must be equal. Therefore, we can equate the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms separately:\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\), we get:\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\nSolving the second equation for \\(b\\), we get:\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\nTherefore, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
